---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { GUMROAD_FINANCE } from "../../consts";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Product",
  name: "Finance Starter Pack for Power BI",
  description:
    "A Power BI finance starter pack available now: two PBIX files and two PBIT templates on one clean model, with a documented 3-measure setup. A 46-measure P&L model is in build.",
  brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: "Power BI Tutorials" },
  releasedEvent: undefined,
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Finance Starter Pack for Power BI"
  description="Power BI finance starter pack: two PBIX files, two PBIT templates, 3 measures and a documented model for $19. A 46-measure P&L version is in build."
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
  </Fragment>

  <article class="product">
    <div class="content-width">

      <span class="page-eyebrow">Template · starter pack available now</span>
      <h1>A finance pack you can open today — a bigger one is in build.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Two Power BI files on one clean finance model: a category performance
        dashboard and the DAX teaching master, plus PBIT versions for your own
        data. Three measures, 108 rows of demo data, and a README that explains
        every column. $19.
      </p>

      <section class="signup" id="status">
        <h2>Available now — $19</h2>
        <p class="signup-sub">
          What is in the zip: <strong>tmpl-finance.pbix</strong> (category
          performance dashboard — 3 KPI cards, category table, category bar
          chart, 1280×720), <strong>p03-dax.pbix</strong> (the DAX teaching
          master with three measures), <strong>tmpl-finance.pbit</strong> and
          <strong>dax-formula-bar-template.pbit</strong> (the same reports as
          templates, empty model, ready for your data), and
          <strong>README.md</strong> (model dictionary).
        </p>
        <p style="margin: 20px 0 8px;">
          <a class="buy-btn btn btn--primary" data-pack="finance-starter-pack" href={GUMROAD_FINANCE} rel="noopener">Buy Power BI Finance Starter Pack · $19 →</a>
        </p>
        <p class="fineprint">
          Checkout is Gumroad — card, Apple Pay, Google Pay and PayPal. The
          download link arrives immediately. If a file will not open or the pack
          does not match this page, email hello@powerbitutorials.com and I will
          fix it.
        </p>
        <p class="fineprint">
          The <strong>46-measure P&amp;L model</strong> described further down is
          the pro version and is still in build. The starter pack above is a
          working subset of it, priced as a starter.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="block">
        <p class="kicker">The pro version · in build</p>
        <h2>Most finance templates break the moment you slice them.</h2>
        <p>
          Templates sold online are usually a PBIX with a nice screenshot and
          three problems underneath: signs are backwards, so gross profit is
          negative; the budget page stops working when someone touches a slicer;
          months sort alphabetically and nobody notices until March.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each of those is cheap to avoid at design time and expensive to fix
          after you have handed the report to a CFO.
        </p>

        <div class="claims">
          <div class="claim">
            <span class="claim-mark">±</span>
            <h3>Signs that survive review</h3>
            <p>Revenue and cost are both stored positive. Gross profit is subtraction, not guesswork.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="claim">
            <span class="claim-mark">⇄</span>
            <h3>Budget that doesn’t argue with slicers</h3>
            <p>Variance measures switch scenario with <code>REMOVEFILTERS</code>, so the page stays correct.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="claim">
            <span class="claim-mark">▤</span>
            <h3>Months that sort</h3>
            <p>Sort-by-column is set in the model, not left to whatever the visual decides.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="block">
        <h2>What you get</h2>
        <ul class="includes">
          <li>
            <strong>The model.</strong> One fact table, five dimensions, one
            marked date table. Every relationship one-to-many, single direction.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>46 measures</strong> in four display folders: core P&amp;L,
            time intelligence, budget and variance, helpers. Commented, not
            cryptic.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Four report pages</strong> — Executive Summary, P&amp;L
            Statement, Variance Analysis, Cost Centre Detail — with layout
            dimensions and field bindings documented.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Data and theme.</strong> 1,728 rows with a fixed seed, a
            script to regenerate or resize them, and a theme JSON so the pack
            looks like one product.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Documentation.</strong> Model spec, page-by-page build guide,
            and the self-check list that catches the mistakes people actually
            make.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="block">
        <h2>Check it before you buy</h2>
        <p>
          Every number in the documentation matches the file. With all filters
          cleared, the sample model returns:
        </p>
        <table class="figures">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Line</th><th>Amount</th><th>% of revenue</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Revenue</td><td>29,126,563</td><td>100.0%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>COGS</td><td>5,901,187</td><td>20.3%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Gross profit</td><td>23,225,376</td><td>79.7%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operating expenses</td><td>13,800,428</td><td>47.4%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>EBITDA</td><td>9,424,948</td><td>32.4%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Net profit</td><td>8,327,138</td><td>28.6%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="caption">
          These are the first item on the self-check list, not a claim in a
          screenshot. The data is synthetic — no real company, no real customers.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="block two-col">
        <div>
          <h2>It’s for you if</h2>
          <ul class="plain">
            <li>you’ve been asked for “a proper P&amp;L” and have two days</li>
            <li>you deliver client reports and need a credible starting model</li>
            <li>you’re moving a monthly pack out of Excel</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2>It’s not for you if</h2>
          <ul class="plain">
            <li>you need a balance sheet or cash flow statement</li>
            <li>you need daily journal-level detail</li>
            <li>you need multi-currency consolidation</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="block faq">
        <h2>Questions</h2>

        <h3>Do I need Power BI Desktop?</h3>
        <p>
          Yes — the free Windows app — or the Power BI Service in a browser. No
          paid add-ons and no custom visuals.
        </p>

        <h3>Do I get a .pbix file?</h3>
        <p>
          In the finished version, yes: a PBIX with the sample data embedded and
          a PBIT for connecting your own source. Neither exists yet — that is
          the remaining work.
        </p>

        <h3>Can I use my own data?</h3>
        <p>
          Yes. Use the <code>.pbit</code> version and swap the CSVs for your
          source, keeping the field names and the sign convention from the model
          spec.
        </p>

        <h3>Why is there no balance sheet?</h3>
        <p>
          Because it needs different sign handling and opening/closing logic.
          Bolting it onto a P&amp;L model makes both halves worse. If the pack
          sells, that is the next one.
        </p>

        <h3>Will it work with my chart of accounts?</h3>
        <p>
          Yes. Accounts live in <code>DimAccount</code>; the measures only depend
          on the six values in <code>L1</code>, so renaming the rest is safe.
        </p>

        <h3>How do I get the files?</h3>
        <p>
          The starter pack is on sale now above — checkout is Gumroad and the
          download link arrives immediately. The 46-measure pro version has no
          checkout yet; the PBIX still has to be assembled and checked on
          Windows before it ships.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="closing">
        <h2>Start from a model that already works.</h2>
        <p>Two days of modelling, or one afternoon of adjusting. Your call.</p>
        <a class="btn btn--primary buy-btn" data-pack="finance-starter-pack" href={GUMROAD_FINANCE} rel="noopener">Get the starter pack · $19</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/resources/time-intelligence-starter/">Or the $9 time-intelligence pack</a>
      </section>

    </div>
  </article>

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .product { padding: 56px 0 88px; }
  .page-eyebrow {
    display: inline-block; font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--c-accent-dark); letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }
  .product h1 { font-size: 2.2rem; line-height: 1.2; margin-bottom: 18px; max-width: 26ch; }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--c-text-muted);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--c-accent); padding-left: 18px;
    margin-bottom: 32px; max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .signup { margin-bottom: 48px; max-width: 620px; }
  .signup h2 { margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 1.35rem; }
  .signup-sub { color: var(--c-text-muted); margin: 0 0 20px; font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .fineprint { font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--c-text-dim); margin: 10px 0 0; }

  .block { margin-bottom: 48px; max-width: 76ch; }
  .block h2 { font-size: 1.35rem; margin: 0 0 14px; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  .block p { color: var(--c-text-muted); line-height: 1.72; margin: 0 0 14px; }

  .claims { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 14px; margin-top: 24px; }
  .claim {
    border: 1px solid var(--c-border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    padding: 16px; background: var(--c-bg-soft);
  }
  .claim-mark {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    width: 30px; height: 30px; border-radius: 7px;
    background: var(--c-accent); color: var(--c-accent-text);
    font-weight: 800; margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .claim h3 { font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0 0 6px; }
  .claim p { font-size: 0.85rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.55; }

  .includes { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
  .includes li {
    padding: 12px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    font-size: 0.94rem; color: var(--c-text-muted); line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .includes li:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  .includes strong { color: var(--c-text); }

  .figures { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 8px 0 10px; font-size: 0.92rem; }
  .figures th {
    text-align: left; font-size: 0.76rem; text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em; color: var(--c-text-dim);
    padding: 8px 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--c-border);
  }
  .figures td { padding: 9px 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--c-border); }
  .figures td:nth-child(2), .figures td:nth-child(3) { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .caption { font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--c-text-dim); }

  .two-col { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 32px; max-width: none; }
  .plain { padding-left: 18px; margin: 0; color: var(--c-text-muted); font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.8; }

  .pricing { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 14px; margin-bottom: 12px; max-width: 560px; }
  .price-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--c-border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 18px;
  }
  .price-card h3 { margin: 0 0 6px; font-size: 1rem; }
  .price-card .amount { font-size: 1.7rem; font-weight: 800; color: var(--c-text); margin: 0 0 6px; }
  .price-card p { margin: 0; font-size: 0.86rem; }

  .faq h3 { font-size: 1rem; margin: 22px 0 6px; }
  .faq p { margin: 0; }

  .closing {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--c-border); padding-top: 28px; margin-top: 8px;
  }
  .closing h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .closing p { color: var(--c-text-muted); margin: 0 0 18px; }
  .btn {
    display: inline-block; padding: 12px 22px; border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--c-accent); color: var(--c-accent-text);
    font-weight: 700; text-decoration: none;
  }
  .btn:hover { background: var(--c-accent-hover); text-decoration: none; }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .claims, .two-col, .pricing { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .product h1 { font-size: 1.75rem; }
  }
</style>

<style is:global>
  .buy-btn {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 12px 20px;
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: var(--c-accent);
    color: var(--c-accent-text);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .buy-btn:hover { opacity: 0.9; }
</style>
